// #189 (PERF-HOF-CARRIER) iterator customer class 1/4, committed per R15.
//
// CLASS: a terminal method directly on a bare `.iter()` receiver.
//
// `iter()` is stamped `Ptr(HeapKind::Iterator)` receiver-invariantly
// (`mir_compiler/types.rs` `well_known_method_return_kind`), so `.count()`'s
// receiver carries that kind and hits the Wave 1b SEAM B refusal at
// `mir_compiler/terminators.rs`: iterator-receiver methods have no sound JIT
// codegen, so the WHOLE function bails to the bytecode interpreter, whose
// iterator handlers are carrier-correct.
//
// Measured at 039be067: `program_fallback` detail contains
// "iterator-receiver method `.count()`". The program is CORRECT — VM == JIT —
// it just never runs native.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS BEFORE THE FIX. The committed corpus contained ZERO
// programs using `.iter()` at all (swept all 468 under `--mode jit`), so the
// VM/JIT differential had no coverage of the carrier the unification is about
// to change. Landing that change against zero coverage, on a carrier path
// whose sibling defect (#219) is an open silent-wrong-output, is the shape
// that produced the 841f92f7 revert. These fixtures pin correctness NOW and
// become the per-class nativity evidence when the unification lands.

let xs = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
print(xs.iter().count())
